          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Kwame Nkrumah's Early Life</strong><br>\nKwame Nkrumah was born on September 21, 1909, in the Gold Coast, now known as Ghana. His early education was at a Catholic mission school, where he became a teacher. He later studied at the Achimota School in Accra, where he trained as a teacher. Nkrumah was known for his intelligence and zeal for knowledge, which led him to further his studies in the United States at Lincoln University, Pennsylvania, in 1935.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Higher Education and Ideals</strong><br>\nIn the United States, Nkrumah was influenced by the ideas of Marcus Garvey and W.E.B. Du Bois. He pursued higher education, earning multiple degrees in theology, education, philosophy, and economics. During his time in America, he became active in the Pan-African movement and was determined to work towards the independence of African nations from colonial rule.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Political Awakening</strong><br>\nNkrumah's political awakening began with his involvement in the African Students Association of America and Canada. He realized that the struggle for African independence was part of a larger global fight against imperialism and racism. Nkrumah's experiences in the U.S. and his exposure to international politics shaped his vision for an independent Ghana.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
