          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Return to the Gold Coast</strong><br>\nAfter 12 years abroad, Nkrumah returned to the Gold Coast in 1947 at the invitation of the United Gold Coast Convention (UGCC). He served as the general secretary of the UGCC and began advocating for independence from British colonial rule. His dynamic leadership quickly made him the face of the Ghanaian independence movement.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Road to Independence</strong><br>\nNkrumah broke away from the UGCC to form the Convention People's Party (CPP) in 1949. The CPP campaigned on a platform of \"self-government now,\" which resonated with the masses. After a series of strikes and unrest, Britain agreed to transfer power. On March 6, 1957, Ghana became the first sub-Saharan African country to gain independence.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Presidency and Vision for Ghana</strong><br>\nAs Ghana's first Prime Minister and then President, Nkrumah initiated ambitious projects to modernize the economy. He championed industrialization, developed infrastructure, and promoted education. His vision was to make Ghana a leading nation in Africa, capable of providing for its own people without foreign aid.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
