          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Pan-Africanism and United Africa</strong><br>\nNkrumah was a staunch advocate of Pan-Africanism and sought to create a united Africa. He believed that only a united continent could achieve true independence and global influence. He worked towards this goal by co-founding the Organization of African Unity (OAU) in 1963.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Overthrow and Exile</strong><br>\nDespite his vision, Nkrumah faced criticism for his authoritarian rule and the declining economy. In 1966, while on a peace mission to Vietnam, he was overthrown by a military coup. Nkrumah lived the rest of his life in exile in Guinea, continuing to write and speak about African unity and development.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Memory</strong><br>\nKwame Nkrumah passed away on April 27, 1972, in Bucharest, Romania. He is remembered as a visionary leader and a driving force behind Ghana's independence. Nkrumah's legacy continues to influence African politics and the global Pan-African movement. His ideas and ambitions for Ghana and Africa remain relevant in discussions about the continent's past, present, and future.</p>\n"
              },
